{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והשקה", :translit "v'hisqah", :gloss "and he shall make drink"} {:he "והשקה את האשה", :translit "v'hisqah et ha-isha", :gloss "and he shall make the woman drink"} {:he "והשקה את נחל השטים", :translit "v'hisqah et nachal ha-shittim", :gloss "and he shall make drink the Valley of Shittim"} {:he "ואד יעלה מן הארץ והשקה", :translit "ve-ed ya'aleh min ha-aretz v'hisqah", :gloss "and a mist went up from the earth and watered"} {:he "שטים", :translit "shittim", :gloss "Shittim / acacia"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["והשקה את האשה" "והשקה את נחל השטים" "ואד יעלה מן הארץ והשקה"]} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay linking the three והשקה verses: dust in water (Genesis 2:6), adultery (Numbers 5), and the Valley of Shittim (Joel 4:18) — the shared root שטים ties them together thematically."}], :frame {:text "The procedure of the sotah requires the priest to take holy water mixed with dust from the Tabernacle floor and make the suspected adulteress drink it (Numbers 5:17, 5:24). Genesis 2:6 describes a mist rising from the earth to water the ground before rainfall existed; Ba'al HaTurim reads that watering as paradigmatic of dust combined with water. Joel 4:18 says a fountain will issue from the House of the LORD and water the Valley of Shittim. The word שטים here functions both as the place name Shittim and carries the connotation of straying (as in the sins of Shittim, Numbers 25:1), linking the adulteress's act to that location.", :sources ["Numbers 5:17" "Numbers 5:24" "Genesis 2:6" "Joel 4:18" "Numbers 25:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["והשקה את האשה" "והשקה את נחל השטים" "ואד יעלה מן הארץ והשקה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "והשקה את האשה", :refs ["Numbers 5:24"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "והשקה את נחל השטים", :refs ["Joel 4:18"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ואד יעלה מן הארץ והשקה", :refs ["Genesis 2:6"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Wordp